Bro, your test result is actually the most important clue.

You proved:

> For these browser/internal provider APIs, sending OpenAI-style `tools` upstream is basically useless.  
> The models ignore native `tools` unless you inject tool instructions into the prompt.

So the production architecture must be:

> **OpenAI-compatible on the outside, prompted tool-output protocol on the inside.**

Meaning:

```text
Client / OpenCode / App
        ↓
OpenAI-compatible API
        ↓
Mirage Gateway
        ↓
Tool prompt compiler
        ↓
Internal browser API adapter
        ↓
Provider web backend
```

The client sees normal OpenAI tool calling.

But internally Mirage does **not** trust the provider’s native tool-calling pipeline unless a provider is explicitly proven to support it.

---

# 1. Core principle for Mirage

## Public API: OpenAI-native

Your users should be able to call:

```http
POST /v1/chat/completions
```

with:

```json
{
  "model": "mirage:deepseek-chat",
  "messages": [...],
  "tools": [...],
  "tool_choice": "auto",
  "stream": true
}
```

They should get back OpenAI-style:

```json
{
  "choices": [
    {
      "message": {
        "role": "assistant",
        "tool_calls": [...]
      },
      "finish_reason": "tool_calls"
    }
  ]
}
```

So from the outside, Mirage looks like OpenAI.

---

## Internal upstream: do not rely on native tools

For the actual provider/browser API, your test shows that this:

```json
{
  "tools": [...]
}
```

is often ignored.

So Mirage should do:

```text
If provider profile.nativeTools === true:
  send native tools upstream
Else:
  strip tools upstream
  convert tools into Mirage tool prompt
  parse tool output from text
```

For almost all your tested browser providers, the mode should be:

```ts
nativeTools: false
promptTools: true
stripUpstreamTools: true
```

---

# 2. Do not make XML the permanent core

Your data says XML injection is currently what makes tool calling work.

That is useful, but I would not make XML the permanent long-term protocol.

Why?

- XML is verbose
- weak models can break tags
- JSON inside XML is annoying
- streaming XML parsing is more fragile
- provider product prompts may interfere
- it feels like a hack layer

Instead, use a custom Mirage protocol.

But do not over-invent.

Use:

> **Custom envelope + JSON payload**

The envelope is Mirage-specific. The payload remains JSON because LLMs are relatively good at JSON and validators are easy.

So the format is not “pure XML”, and not “pure JSON response”. It is a Mirage block.

---

# 3. Mirage Tool Protocol v1 — MTP/1

Use this as the internal tool-output format.

## Tool call block

```text
[MIRAGE_TOOL_CALL_V1]
{
  "id": "call_001",
  "name": "write_file",
  "arguments": {
    "name": "hello.txt",
    "content": "Hello, world!"
  }
}
[/MIRAGE_TOOL_CALL_V1]
```

This is simple.

It is not XML.

It is not just JSON.

It is a custom Mirage block containing JSON.

---

## Tool result block

When returning tool results to the model, use:

```text
[MIRAGE_TOOL_RESULT_V1]
{
  "id": "call_001",
  "output": {
    "status": "success"
  },
  "error": null
}
[/MIRAGE_TOOL_RESULT_V1]
```

Then ask the model to continue.

---

## Multiple tool calls

For now, prefer one tool call per turn.

If you later allow parallel calls, use repeated blocks:

```text
[MIRAGE_TOOL_CALL_V1]
{
  "id": "call_001",
  "name": "read_file",
  "arguments": {
    "path": "a.txt"
  }
}
[/MIRAGE_TOOL_CALL_V1]

[MIRAGE_TOOL_CALL_V1]
{
  "id": "call_002",
  "name": "read_file",
  "arguments": {
    "path": "b.txt"
  }
}
[/MIRAGE_TOOL_CALL_V1]
```

But for weak/free models, default to:

```text
one tool call only
```

That will be much more robust.

