# 9. Streaming architecture

This is where most projects die.

You need a streaming parser that can handle normal text and tool blocks without breaking the user experience.

---

## Desired behavior

If the model is producing normal text:

```text
The capital of France is Paris.
```

Stream it immediately.

If the model starts producing:

```text
[MIRAGE_TOOL_CALL_V1]
```

then Mirage should pause text emission and buffer the block.

When the closing marker appears:

```text
[/MIRAGE_TOOL_CALL_V1]
```

parse and validate.

If valid, emit OpenAI-compatible `tool_calls`.

If invalid, either repair or return an error depending on policy.

---

## Stream parser state machine

Use a simple state machine:

```ts
type ParserState =
  | "TEXT"
  | "MAYBE_TOOL_START"
  | "TOOL_BLOCK"
  | "TOOL_END_MAYBE";
```

But for implementation, a buffered approach is easier.

---

## Basic streaming parser concept

```ts
const TOOL_START = "[MIRAGE_TOOL_CALL_V1]";
const TOOL_END = "[/MIRAGE_TOOL_CALL_V1]";

class MirageStreamParser {
  private buffer = "";
  private insideTool = false;

  push(chunk: string) {
    this.buffer += chunk;

    const events = [];

    while (true) {
      if (!this.insideTool) {
        const startIndex = this.buffer.indexOf(TOOL_START);

        if (startIndex === -1) {
          const safe = this.getSafeText();

          if (safe) {
            events.push({ type: "text", text: safe });
            this.buffer = this.buffer.slice(safe.length);
          }

          break;
        } else {
          const before = this.buffer.slice(0, startIndex);

          if (before) {
            events.push({ type: "text", text: before });
          }

          this.buffer = this.buffer.slice(startIndex);
          this.insideTool = true;
        }
      } else {
        const endIndex = this.buffer.indexOf(TOOL_END);

        if (endIndex === -1) {
          break;
        } else {
          const block = this.buffer.slice(0, endIndex + TOOL_END.length);

          events.push({
            type: "tool_block",
            raw: block
          });

          this.buffer = this.buffer.slice(endIndex + TOOL_END.length);
          this.insideTool = false;
        }
      }
    }

    return events;
  }

  private getSafeText(): string {
    const holdback = TOOL_START.length;

    if (this.buffer.length <= holdback) {
      return "";
    }

    return this.buffer.slice(0, this.buffer.length - holdback);
  }

  flush() {
    if (this.insideTool) {
      return [
        {
          type: "tool_block_incomplete",
          raw: this.buffer
        }
      ];
    }

    return this.buffer
      ? [
          {
            type: "text",
            text: this.buffer
          }
        ]
      : [];
  }
}
```

This is simple, fast, and robust enough.

