# 10. Parsing the MTP block

Once you extract:

```text
[MIRAGE_TOOL_CALL_V1]
{
  "id": "call_001",
  "name": "write_file",
  "arguments": {
    "name": "hello.txt",
    "content": "Hello, world!"
  }
}
[/MIRAGE_TOOL_CALL_V1]
```

Strip markers:

```ts
function extractToolPayload(raw: string) {
  return raw
    .replace("[MIRAGE_TOOL_CALL_V1]", "")
    .replace("[/MIRAGE_TOOL_CALL_V1]", "")
    .trim();
}
```

Then JSON parse:

```ts
function parseToolBlock(raw: string) {
  const payload = extractToolPayload(raw);

  try {
    const parsed = JSON.parse(payload);

    return {
      ok: true,
      data: parsed
    };
  } catch (error) {
    return {
      ok: false,
      error: String(error),
      rawPayload: payload
    };
  }
}
```

Then validate.

---

# 11. Validation is non-negotiable

Use Zod, Ajv, Valibot, Pydantic, or whatever fits your stack.

Example with Zod:

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

const MirageToolCallSchema = z.object({
  id: z.string().optional(),
  name: z.string(),
  arguments: z.record(z.unknown())
});
```

Then validate against the actual tool schema.

Example:

```ts
function validateToolCall(toolCall: any, tool: MirageToolDefinition) {
  if (toolCall.name !== tool.name) {
    return {
      ok: false,
      error: `Invalid tool name: ${toolCall.name}`
    };
  }

  // Validate arguments against tool.parameters here
  // Use Ajv/Zod/Valibot

  return {
    ok: true
  };
}
```

If invalid, trigger repair.

---

# 12. Repair loop

Do not immediately fail.

If the model gives invalid output, send a repair request.

Example repair prompt:

```text
Your previous response contained an invalid Mirage tool block.

Error:
- arguments.content is required

Original block:
[MIRAGE_TOOL_CALL_V1]
{ ... invalid ... }
[/MIRAGE_TOOL_CALL_V1]

Respond again with only a valid Mirage tool block.
Do not add explanation.
```

Production policy:

```ts
repairAttempts: 1
```

or maybe:

```ts
repairAttempts: 2
```

But do not retry forever.

