# 17. Built-in provider tools problem

This is one of your biggest issues.

The provider’s own web app may inject tools like:

```text
search
browser
code
canvas
files
retrieval
```

Mirage must suppress or isolate them.

---

## Strategy A: Namespace Mirage tools

For polluted providers, internally rename tools.

Client tool:

```text
write_file
```

Mirage prompt tool:

```text
mirage_write_file
```

Then when the model outputs:

```json
{
  "name": "mirage_write_file"
}
```

Mirage maps it back to:

```text
write_file
```

for the OpenAI response.

This reduces collisions with provider tools.

Use this when:

```ts
quirks.builtinToolConfusion === true
```

---

## Strategy B: Reject non-Mirage tools

If the model outputs:

```json
{
  "name": "web_search"
}
```

and `web_search` is not a Mirage tool, do not execute it.

Send repair:

```text
Invalid tool name: web_search.
Only Mirage tools are available.
Use one of the following tools: write_file.
```

---

## Strategy C: Final reminder

For weak models, append a final reminder at the end of the prompt:

```text
Reminder: If you need to use a tool, output only a [MIRAGE_TOOL_CALL_V1] block. Do not use built-in tools.
```

This helps a lot.

