# 27. Tool compiler example

Instead of dumping raw schemas, compile tools like this:

```ts
function compileToolsForPrompt(tools: MirageToolDefinition[]) {
  return tools
    .map((tool, index) => {
      const lines = [];

      lines.push(`${index + 1}. ${tool.name}`);

      if (tool.description) {
        lines.push(`Description: ${tool.description}`);
      }

      lines.push("Arguments:");

      const properties = tool.parameters.properties || {};
      const required = new Set(tool.parameters.required || []);

      for (const [key, schema] of Object.entries(properties)) {
        const type = (schema as any).type || "string";
        const isRequired = required.has(key) ? "required" : "optional";
        const description = (schema as any).description;

        lines.push(
          `- ${key}: ${type}, ${isRequired}${
            description ? `. ${description}` : ""
          }`
        );
      }

      return lines.join("\n");
    })
    .join("\n\n");
}
```

For `write_file`, this becomes:

```text
1. write_file
Description: Write a file.
Arguments:
- name: string, required. The file name.
- content: string, required. The file content.
```

This is much better for weak models.

